.Dd March 9, 2026
.Dt ZFS-EVENTS 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-events
.Nd display file-level event history for ZFS datasets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfs
.Cm events
.Op Fl jHp
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Op Fl o Ar object
.Op Fl t Ar @snapshot Ns Op Ar ..@snapshot
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Op Ar path
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm zfs Cm events
command displays the file-level event history for a ZFS dataset.
Events are logged when files and directories are created, modified, renamed,
or deleted, providing a complete audit trail of filesystem changes.
.Pp
Events are stored in an internal ring buffer within each dataset and are
keyed by ZFS object ID, ensuring that the history of a file is preserved
even after renames.
The event log is included in ZFS send streams and travels with the pool,
making it useful for replication conflict detection and forensic analysis.
.Pp
To enable event logging on a dataset, set the
.Sy events
property:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs set events=on tank/data
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Sy events
property is inheritable, so enabling it on a parent dataset will enable
event logging for all descendant datasets.
.
.Ss Event Types
The following event types are recorded:
.Bl -tag -width "TRUNCATE" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy CREATE
File or directory created
.It Sy REMOVE
File or directory deleted
.It Sy RENAME
File or directory renamed (includes old and new paths)
.It Sy LINK
Hard link created
.It Sy SYMLINK
Symbolic link created
.It Sy TRUNCATE
File truncated
.It Sy SETATTR
File attributes changed (permissions, ownership, times)
.El
.Pp
Note that individual
.Xr write 2
operations are not logged, as this would generate excessive events.
File modifications can be inferred from
.Sy SETATTR
events that update
.Sy mtime ,
or by comparing snapshots.
.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-t @snap..@snap"
.It Fl j
Output in JSON format for machine parsing.
Each event is printed as a separate JSON object on its own line.
.It Fl H
Scripted mode: do not print headers and separate fields by a single tab
instead of arbitrary whitespace.
.It Fl p
Display numbers in exact (parsable) values rather than human-readable form.
Timestamps are shown as Unix epoch seconds.
.It Fl n Ar count
Limit output to the most recent
.Ar count
events.
By default, all events in the ring buffer are displayed.
.It Fl o Ar object
Display only events for the specified ZFS object ID.
Object IDs can be obtained from
.Xr zdb 8
or from previous
.Nm zfs Cm events
output.
.It Fl t Ar @snapshot Ns Op Ar ..@snapshot
Display events relative to snapshots.
With a single snapshot, display events that occurred after that snapshot.
With a range
.Ar @snap1..@snap2 ,
display events between the two snapshots.
.It Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
The dataset to query for events.
.It Ar path
Optional path to filter events.
Only events affecting the specified file or directory will be shown.
For renamed files, events are matched against both old and new paths.
.El
.
.Sh OUTPUT
By default, the following columns are displayed:
.Bl -tag -width "OLD_PATH" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy TXG
Transaction group in which the event occurred
.It Sy TIME
Timestamp of the event
.It Sy OBJECT
ZFS object ID of the affected file
.It Sy EVENT
Type of event (CREATE, REMOVE, RENAME, etc.)
.It Sy PATH
Path to the affected file
.It Sy OLD_PATH
For RENAME events, the previous path
.El
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1: Viewing recent events on a dataset
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events tank/data
TXG      TIME                 OBJECT  EVENT    PATH
1847     2026-03-09 10:15:23  128     CREATE   /tank/data/newfile.txt
1848     2026-03-09 10:16:01  128     SETATTR  /tank/data/newfile.txt
1850     2026-03-09 10:18:45  128     RENAME   /tank/data/renamed.txt
                                               (was: /tank/data/newfile.txt)
1852     2026-03-09 10:20:12  129     CREATE   /tank/data/another.txt
1855     2026-03-09 10:25:33  128     REMOVE   /tank/data/renamed.txt
.Ed
.Ss Example 2: Getting the history of a specific file
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events tank/data /tank/data/important.txt
TXG      TIME                 OBJECT  EVENT    PATH
1200     2026-03-01 09:00:00  256     CREATE   /tank/data/important.txt
1205     2026-03-01 09:15:00  256     SETATTR  /tank/data/important.txt
1340     2026-03-05 14:30:00  256     RENAME   /tank/data/important.txt
                                               (was: /tank/data/draft.txt)
.Ed
.Ss Example 3: JSON output for scripting
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events -j -n 1 tank/data
{"txg":1855,"time":1741518333,"object":128,"event":"REMOVE",
 "path":"/tank/data/renamed.txt","uid":1000,"gid":1000,"mode":33188}
.Ed
.Ss Example 4: Events since a snapshot
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events -t @yesterday tank/data
TXG      TIME                 OBJECT  EVENT    PATH
1847     2026-03-09 10:15:23  128     CREATE   /tank/data/newfile.txt
\&...
.Ed
.Ss Example 5: Events between two snapshots
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events -t @monday..@friday tank/data
.Ed
.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width "ZFS_EVENTS_MAX"
.It Ev ZFS_EVENTS_MAX
Override the default maximum number of events to display.
.El
.
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Evolving .
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-diff 8 ,
.Xr zfs-set 8 ,
.Xr zfsprops 7 ,
.Xr zmetad 8 ,
.Xr zpool-features 7
.
.Sh NOTES
The event log is stored as a ring buffer within the dataset.
When the buffer is full, the oldest events are overwritten.
The size of the buffer is controlled by the
.Sy events_size
property, which defaults to 1 MiB.
.Pp
Events are recorded within the same transaction group as the operation
that triggered them, ensuring consistency with snapshots.
.Pp
The
.Sy org.openzfs:events
pool feature must be enabled for event logging to function.
The feature is automatically activated when
.Sy events=on
is first set on any dataset in the pool.
.Pp
For long-term event retention beyond the ring buffer capacity, use
.Xr zmetad 8
to export events to an external SQLite database.
